      'cp.h.serve': 'cd latent-atlas\n./serve.sh          # or: python3 -m http.server 8765\n# open http://localhost:8765',
      'cp.h.mlx': ['Running a local MLX model on a Mac (Apple Silicon)', 'mlx-lm (from Apple/ml-explore) needs Python ≥ 3.10 — the python3 that ships with macOS is 3.9. Install it in one of these ways:', 'Start the OpenAI-compatible server (port 8080, CORS open by default). The first time, it downloads the model from Hugging Face:', 'Or use the script that ships with the platform — it picks the model from your Mac’s RAM, installs mlx-lm if missing and starts the server:', 'Here in the copilot: Base URL http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1, empty API key, empty Model. Click “Test connection”.', 'Verified suggestions by memory — 8 GB: Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507-4bit (2.3 GB); 16–24 GB: gemma-4-12B-it-qat-OptiQ-4bit (8.9 GB) or gemma-4-12B-it-4bit (6.8 GB); 32 GB: gemma-4-26b-a4b-it-4bit (15.4 GB, MoE); 48 GB: Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-4bit (20.4 GB); 64 GB+: Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-8bit (37.7 GB). Rule: weights + ~10% slack ≤ 90% of the GPU budget (≈ 2/3 of RAM up to 36 GB, 3/4 above). The MLX button in the top bar does all of this for you: it suggests, downloads, starts and connects the model. The quantization chapter explains the “4bit”/“MXFP4” in the name.', 'Models with “thinking” can spend the whole budget reasoning: turn on “Ask for answers without thinking” or raise the token limit. If you open the platform via file:// and something fails, serve it over HTTP:'],
